Shoals.Stochastic: the step count must be at least 1, got -8; with no steps the evolution loop never runs, so the sampler would return s0 and v0 unchanged for a horizon it did not simulate
Assumption: a negative step count is not a quantity, so there is no reading on
which an answer exists. Returning a confident s0 for it is a wrong answer
dressed as an answer. This is the unarguable half of the step-count
precondition and the reason the defect was filed; the zero case is the policy
question that came with it, and it has its own file.

Measured on the unguarded tree at s0 = 100, v0 = 0.04, t = 1.0, seed 7:
`n_steps = -8` returned 100.00001 for the terminal spot and 0.04 for the
terminal variance -- byte-identical to `n_steps = 0` -- while `n_steps = 64`
returned 146.3306.

The diagnostic names the received value rather than only the rule. A step
count is one of two integer arguments a caller passes to this sampler, and a
message that says only "must be at least 1" leaves them to work out which
argument the compiler meant.

What this fixture does NOT assert: it does not pin the zero step count, which
has its own file because its refusal rests on a different warrant, and it does
not pin what stays admitted. `n_steps = 1` is the extremal admitted value and
is pinned by tests/stochastic.ch.

Deleting the checked_step_count call from THIS sampler does not make the
fixture pass: the assertion is one that s0 also fails, so the file would then
fail on the assertion rather than on the refusal, `--expect neg` would see the
wrong text, and it would report WRONG-DIAGNOSTIC. Deleting the guard from
heston_qe_paths_terminal leaves this fixture green, which is why there is one
file per entry point rather than one for the module.
